Every method takes either \code{n} or \code{frac}, except \code{pps_cps}, which requires
\code{n}. For fixed-size methods, \code{frac} follows the \code{round} parameter (ceiling
by default). Bernoulli and PPS Poisson use an unrounded expected target.
\code{prn} in the last column marks the methods that accept permanent random
numbers for coordination, and it is always optional. \code{cube} takes \code{aux}
optionally, whereas \code{lpm2} and \code{scps} require \code{spread}.
\subsection{What each method does}{
\itemize{
\item \code{srswor}, the default, is simple random sampling.
\item \code{srswr} allows a unit to be drawn more than once.
\item \code{systematic} selects at a fixed interval.
\item \code{bernoulli} makes an independent trial for each unit.
\item \code{pps_systematic} is order-sensitive, with exact first-order
probabilities. Sorted with \code{control}, it spreads the sample over the
sorted variables.
\item \code{pps_brewer} is Brewer's general method for any \code{n}, with exact
first-order and approximate joint probabilities. \code{PPS_BREWER} in SAS
and SPSS selects two units per stratum.
\item \code{pps_cps} has the highest entropy and exact joint probabilities.
\item \code{pps_sampford} has exact Sampford joint probabilities. It is the
\code{PPS_SAMPFORD} method of SAS and SPSS.
\item \code{pps_poisson} is the PPS analog of Bernoulli sampling.
\item \code{pps_sps} (sequential Poisson) and \code{pps_pareto} meet their probability
targets only approximately.
\item \code{pps_multinomial} allows any number of hits, with the Hansen-Hurwitz
estimator.
\item \code{pps_chromy} is sequential and order-sensitive, the method of SAS
\code{PPS_SEQ}.
\item \code{pps_systematic_mr} is systematic PPS without the cap at 1, the method
of SAS \code{PPS_SYS}. \code{pps_systematic} caps each chance at 1 and takes a
large unit once, whereas \code{pps_systematic_mr} hits it once per sampling
interval its size spans.
\item \code{cube} is the cube method of Deville and \enc{Tillé}{Tille} (2004).
\item \code{lpm2} and \code{scps} spread the sample in space.
}

\code{Minimum} in the Replacement column is probability minimum replacement.
\code{pps_chromy} and \code{pps_systematic_mr} draw a unit either
\eqn{\lfloor E \rfloor}{floor(E)} or \eqn{\lceil E \rceil}{ceiling(E)}
times, where \eqn{E} is its expected number of hits, so a unit is never hit
more often than its size warrants. When every \eqn{E} is below 1,
\code{pps_systematic_mr} draws the units \code{pps_systematic} draws from the same
seed.
}

\subsection{Choosing a PPS method}{

When a protocol names the method, use that method.

Otherwise a general fixed-size method is the usual choice. \code{pps_brewer},
\code{pps_cps} and \code{pps_sampford} select any number of units, ignore the frame
order and give every pair of units a positive joint probability, so the
variance can be estimated from the design. The joint probabilities are
exact for CPS and Sampford and approximate for Brewer.

\code{pps_systematic}, \code{pps_systematic_mr} and \code{pps_chromy} depend on the
frame order. Sorted with \code{control}, they spread the sample over the
sorted variables, which is why many household survey protocols use them.
Both systematic methods can give some pairs of units a joint probability
of zero, which rules out a design-unbiased variance estimator, and a
frame whose order resonates with the sampling interval can make their
standard errors far too small. \code{\link[=as_svydesign]{as_svydesign()}} uses Brewer's
approximation for a \code{pps_systematic} stage, treats a \code{pps_systematic_mr}
stage as drawn with replacement, and warns for both. A \code{pps_chromy}
stage is treated as drawn with replacement too (\link{variance_estimation}).

\code{pps_poisson}, \code{pps_sps} and \code{pps_pareto} take permanent random numbers,
for samples coordinated across occasions or surveys.
}

\subsection{Spreading the sample}{

A protocol that sorts the frame by geography wants the sample spread over
the areas it sorts by. samplyr reaches that spread in four ways, and each
pays for it differently.
\itemize{
\item \code{control} with \code{pps_systematic}, \code{pps_systematic_mr} or \code{pps_chromy}
gives implicit stratification, with the variance limits given above.
\item Finer strata in \code{\link[=stratify_by]{stratify_by()}}, down to two units per stratum, with a
general method. Every joint probability stays positive, at the cost of
more strata and smaller takes in each.
\item \code{method = "cube"} with \code{aux = c(bound(province))} fixes the count in
each province to an integer adjacent to its expected count.
\item \code{method = "lpm2"} or \code{"scps"} with \code{spread} spreads the sample in space.
}

The last two keep exact first-order probabilities, but their joint
probabilities are refused and their variance comes from \code{subbootstrap} or
\code{mrbbootstrap} replicates only (\link{variance_estimation}). No method
dominates the others, and the choice depends on the variance the analysis
needs.
}

\subsection{Selection and inference}{

Every method above draws a sample when its inputs are met, but selection
support and variance support are separate contracts. \link{variance_estimation}
gives how each method's variance is estimated and how exact it is.

SPS, Pareto and custom approximate targets require
\code{allow_approximate = TRUE} in \code{\link[=exante_probabilities]{exante_probabilities()}} and
\code{\link[=exante_overlaps]{exante_overlaps()}}. Estimates using these targets need not be
design-unbiased. Unknown probabilities are refused. Probability and
variance declarations by a custom-method author are contracts, not proofs.

The two order-sampling methods part ways in small pools. Their realized
inclusion probabilities depart from the targets as the take shrinks,
and those of \code{pps_pareto} stay closer to them than those of \code{pps_sps},
so an estimator built on the targets is less biased. For coordinated
samples from small pools, prefer \code{pps_pareto}.
}

\subsection{Fixed vs random sample size}{

Where the table says \strong{Fixed}, \code{n} is the realized sample size. Where it
says \strong{Random}, \code{n} is the \emph{expected} size: it is converted to
\code{frac = n / N} (with \code{N} the stratum or frame size) and the realized count
varies around it.

For \code{pps_poisson}, the raw inclusion probabilities are computed as
\eqn{\pi_i = f \cdot x_i / \bar{x}}{pi_i = f * x_i / mean(x)} where
\eqn{f} is \code{frac} and \eqn{x_i} is the MOS value. Any \eqn{\pi_i > 1}
is clipped to 1, so the expected sample size
\eqn{E[n] = \sum \min(\pi_i, 1)}{E[n] = sum(min(pi_i, 1))} can be less
than \eqn{f \cdot N}{f * N} when large units dominate the MOS
distribution. Use \code{certainty_size} or \code{certainty_prop} to handle these
dominant units explicitly.

Declaring certainty units does more than remove them. The remainder is
re-resolved over the reduced target and the reduced MOS total, so the
surviving chances can rise. The remaining expected take equals the reduced target
only if no remaining probability needs clipping. See \code{\link[=draw]{draw()}} for the
certainty-adjusted \code{n} and \code{frac} contract.

This is not silent. \code{execute()} warns with class
\code{samplyr_warning_poisson_shortfall} once per stage when a pool's resolved
expectation falls more than 5\% below what that pool could reach, naming the
pools affected and how many chances were clipped. The comparison is against
the reachable target rather than the request: a pool asked for more units
than it holds has already had its target reduced by the population, which
\code{samplyr_warning_nominal_cap} reports, and only the further reduction that
saturation caused is charged here. A design reduced both ways gets both
warnings.

The shortfall is a gap between the nominal and realized design, not a bias.
Horvitz-Thompson estimates from a saturated Poisson design remain unbiased,
because the weights are the reciprocals of the resolved probabilities.

When an allocation method is set in \code{\link[=stratify_by]{stratify_by()}} (\code{equal},
\code{proportional}, \code{neyman}, \code{optimal}, \code{power}), specify total sample size via \code{n}.
Combining \code{alloc} with \code{frac} is not supported.
}

\subsection{Registered methods}{

A method registered with \code{\link[sondage:register_method]{sondage::register_method()}} is named
\code{"pps_<name>"} for \code{type = "wor"} or \code{type = "wr"}, and
\code{"balanced_<name>"} for \code{type = "balanced"}, where \code{mos} is optional.
\code{supports_aux = TRUE} permits ordinary balancing variables in \code{aux}, and
\code{supports_spread = TRUE} requires coordinates in \code{spread}. To draw such
a function without coordinates, register it again under another name,
without \code{supports_spread = TRUE}. A method
declaring both takes \code{aux} and \code{spread} together, for a sample that is
balanced and well spread. Under \code{\link[=stratify_by]{stratify_by()}}, a method declaring
\code{supports_strata = TRUE} draws all strata in one call, as \code{"cube"} does,
and any other registered method draws each stratum on its own.

Its weights are \code{1 / pik}, the inclusion expectation (target inclusion
probabilities, or expected hits for \code{type = "wr"}) samplyr hands to the
method. The \code{probabilities} tier declared at registration is \code{"exact"}
(the design's first-order inclusion probabilities, or expected hits,
equal \code{pik}), \code{"approximate"} (to a documented approximation, as in
Pareto sampling), or \code{"unknown"}, the default. \code{\link[=draw]{draw()}} refuses
\code{"unknown"}, because \code{pik} is then a selection weight only and the
weights would be systematically biased. The classic trap is
\code{sample(prob = pik)}. With \code{replace = TRUE} its expected hits equal
\code{pik}, a valid \code{type = "wr"} method, but its inclusion probabilities
without replacement do not, so a \code{type = "wor"} wrapper's tier is
unknown.
}
}
